# core.autocrlf is true on the Windows dev machine, so anything checked out here
# gets CRLF by default. That is fine for files that stay on Windows and fatal for
# a shell script that gets copied to the Pi and run: bash reports
# `$'\r': command not found` on the first line, which reads like a corrupt file
# rather than a line-ending problem.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning in reverse - these only ever run on Windows.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
